Multiple workheads in additive manufacturing enables new possibilities for multiple material printing and printjob division. 
In this study, several adhered boundary surface patterns were tested to parameterize the bonding between materials at the joint interface. The method involves changing layer at the boundary while keeping both FFF printhead continuously printing, which is only achievable with a setup which has multiple printhead carriages mounted on the same z-stage. such a printer setup could also provide control over the bonding interval and be optimized to achieve better bonding strength by arranging the bonding timing according to the thermal properties of extruded material. 
Focusing on developing a perameteric model to generalize and pridict the microstructure at the joint interface for various boundary surface patterns. Optimize path planning parameters for each pattern and compare the adhesion strength by testing the tensile strength of multiple speciments printed with different settings. 


---

Advances in multi-material additive manufacturing have created new opportunities for fabricating components with spatially varying material properties. This study investigates the interfacial bonding behavior in fused filament fabrication (FFF) systems equipped with multiple printheads operating on a shared z-stage. By maintaining continuous extrusion during layer transitions at material boundaries, this setup enables precise control of bonding intervals and thermal interaction at the joint interface.

A parametric model would be developed to characterize the relationship between input surface geometry, extrusion parameters, and resulting microstructure of the interfacial joint. Several boundary surface patterns along vertical plane were experimentally tested to quantify adhesion performance under controlled layer-change conditions. Tensile strength measurements of printed specimens were used to assess the influence of printing path parameters and bonding timing on adhesion quality.

The findings provide a framework for predicting and optimizing inter-material joint performance in multi-printhead FFF systems. This work contributes to establishing design and process guidelines for achieving strong, tunable interfaces in multi-material additive manufacturing, supporting applications in structural, functional, and gradient-material components.